WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.803
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.103 --> 00:00:09.547
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:17.062
Unit twenty-five: Personal Finance News. Today you will give advice with should, ought to and had better.

4
00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:20.026
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.838
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:26.510
Budget.

7
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:30.317
Budget means a plan for how to spend and save money.

8
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:33.381
A monthly budget helps control spending.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.194
Your turn. Say: Budget.

10
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:39.883
Budget.

11
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.605
Savings.

12
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:45.580
Savings means money that has been saved and not spent.

13
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.345
Her savings paid for university.

14
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.672
Your turn. Say: Savings.

15
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:55.674
Savings.

16
00:00:56.417 --> 00:00:57.801
Compound interest.

17
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:03.986
Compound interest means interest that is calculated on both the original money and earlier interest.

18
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:07.338
Compound interest makes savings grow faster.

19
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.136
Your turn. Say: Compound interest.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.907
Compound interest.

21
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.291
Debt.

22
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.144
Debt means money that someone owes.

23
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.703
Paying off debt should come first.

24
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:26.155
Your turn. Say: Debt.

25
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.850
Debt.

26
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:31.653
Investment.

27
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.845
Investment means money put into something to make a profit.

28
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.378
A long-term investment can grow for decades.

29
00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:42.194
Your turn. Say: Investment.

30
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:46.162
Investment.

31
00:01:46.917 --> 00:01:48.185
Emergency fund.

32
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:52.820
Emergency fund means money saved for unexpected problems.

33
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.032
An emergency fund covers sudden repairs.

34
00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:59.966
Your turn. Say: Emergency fund.

35
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.300
Emergency fund.

36
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.492
Financial literacy.

37
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:11.445
Financial literacy means the knowledge needed to manage money well.

38
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.396
Financial literacy helps families avoid debt.

39
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:19.123
Your turn. Say: Financial literacy.

40
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:23.328
Financial literacy.

41
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:24.957
Goal.

42
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.263
Goal means something you plan to achieve.

43
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.815
A clear goal keeps you saving.

44
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.628
Your turn. Say: Goal.

45
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.352
Goal.

46
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:41.992
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:50.692
Visal, a finance column says 'You ought to build an emergency fund, and you had better avoid high-interest debt.' How do these differ?

48
00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:59.277
Ought to and should give general advice. Had better is stronger and suggests a bad result if you ignore it.

49
00:03:00.383 --> 00:03:02.000
Can I use them in the negative?

50
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:10.208
Yes: shouldn't, ought not to, had better not. 'You had better not spend your savings on a risky investment.'

51
00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:13.548
What is the best advice for beginners?

52
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:22.480
Write a budget, set a goal and use compound interest. Financial literacy will give you more choices later.

53
00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:25.227
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:30.591
Grammar focus: Giving advice: should and ought to and had better.

55
00:03:30.891 --> 00:03:32.147
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.559
You should write a monthly budget.

57
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.111
Families ought to build an emergency fund.

58
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:41.864
You had better not ignore high-interest debt.

59
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:52.177
Should and ought to + base verb give advice. Had better + base verb is stronger, and its negative is had better not. All three are followed by the base verb.

60
00:03:52.917 --> 00:03:56.103
Be careful! Don't say: You had better saving more.

61
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:00.368
Say: You had better save more.

62
00:04:00.668 --> 00:04:02.807
Use the base verb after had better.

63
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.496
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.916
Money Tips for Beginners.

65
00:04:09.216 --> 00:04:11.855
Good money habits begin with a simple budget.

66
00:04:12.867 --> 00:04:17.792
Experts say you should save a part of every income and ought to build an emergency fund first.

67
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.495
You had better pay off high-interest debt before you start an investment.

68
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.739
Compound interest then rewards patient savers, because savings grow on top of earlier interest.

69
00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:34.183
A clear goal and strong financial literacy make every decision easier.

70
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:39.384
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.133
You blank write a monthly budget.

72
00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:46.377
A, should. B, should to. C, must to.

73
00:04:49.933 --> 00:04:51.828
The answer is A: should.

74
00:04:52.983 --> 00:04:55.819
Families ought blank build an emergency fund.

75
00:04:56.119 --> 00:04:59.238
A, to. B, -ing. C, not.

76
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.370
The answer is A: to.

77
00:05:05.517 --> 00:05:08.747
Interest calculated on earlier interest is blank interest.

78
00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:12.491
A, compound. B, simple. C, debt.

79
00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:18.381
The answer is A: compound.

80
00:05:19.517 --> 00:05:21.284
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:21.584 --> 00:05:27.467
A, You had better to save.. B, You had better save.. C, You had better saving..

82
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.592
The answer is B: You had better save..

83
00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:38.537
According to the reading, you should pay off high-interest debt before…

84
00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:42.119
A, investing. B, sleeping. C, budgeting.

85
00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:47.678
The answer is A: investing.

86
00:05:48.867 --> 00:05:51.763
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:52.617 --> 00:05:55.150
What is your most important financial goal?

88
00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:05.381
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:05.681 --> 00:06:27.692
My most important financial goal is to build an emergency fund and then save for university. I should write a budget so that I know where my money goes, and I ought to avoid unnecessary debt. After that, I would like to begin a small investment. Compound interest means that starting early will help me a lot.

90
00:06:27.992 --> 00:06:29.667
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:36.000
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and giving advice: should and ought to and had better.

92
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.112
Today's value is planning ahead. Make a plan and take small steps every day.

93
00:06:41.412 --> 00:06:48.850
Congratulations! You have finished Online Newspaper Class, level two. See you in Online Newspaper Class, level three!

94
00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:52.360
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

